\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{An invariant connection probability with a negative diagonal}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take the marked fourth-root connection #{U} on the
#{4\times4} grid of \ref{fdip-019G}. Its orbit under #{b}
and #{\theta_s} has #{4} members. The single marked spatial
link in #{U,\theta_s U,bU,hU}, respectively, has base and
value
##{((0,1),\ui),\quad ((0,3),\ui),\quad
 ((3,3),-\ui),\quad ((3,1),-\ui).}
The orbit is a finite measurable connection class. Its
uniform probability is invariant under both maps, which
permute its members.}

\p{Define the real observable #{q} to be #{+1} when the
interior time-one slice contains a nonidentity spatial link
and #{-1} otherwise. It depends only on the positive half.
At every point #{V} of the orbit exactly one of #{V} and
#{\theta_s V} has its marked link in that slice. Thus
#{q(\theta_s V)q(V)=-1} pointwise and #{B_+(q,q)=-1}.
By \ref{fdip-019N} the transported diagonal value on the
negative half is also #{-1}.}

\p{For a mixed value, let #{f(V)} read the spatial link at
#{(0,1)} and let #{g(V)} equal one plus twice the spatial
link at #{(3,1)}. Both belong to the positive algebra.
In the displayed orbit order the #{4} values of
#{\overline{f(\theta_s V)}g(V)} are #{3,-3\ui,3,1-2\ui}.
Their average is #{(7-5\ui)/4}. The same value is obtained
on the negative half after pullback by #{b}, or on the
positive half after pullback by #{h}.}
